  "worked_solution": "First, since  $s=4{t}^{3/2}$,  we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =4{t}^{3/2} \\\\ {t}^{3/2} & =\\frac{s}{4} \\\\ t & ={(\\frac{s}{4})}^{2/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe vector function that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r({(\\frac{s}{4})}^{2/3}) \\\\  & =[6{(\\frac{s}{4})}^{2/3}-6]\\,i+4{[{(\\frac{s}{4})}^{2/3}-1]}^{3/2}\\,j+2\\,k \\\\  & =6[{(\\frac{s}{4})}^{2/3}-1]\\,i+4{[{(\\frac{s}{4})}^{2/3}-1]}^{3/2}\\,j+2\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the $x$-component of $R$ is\n\n$$\n6[{(\\frac{s}{4})}^{2/3}-1].\n$$",
